WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.170
I see that all the time.

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:03.550
I have never found that
to be useful advice.

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:05.240
Forget the first hundred words.

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:07.690
You can't treat random people as tutors.

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:12.489
Today, I want to talk about 25
tips to learn a foreign language.

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:17.269
This is based on a blog
post written by Mark Manson.

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:20.500
Someone pointed out that this
was a, an interesting collection

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:22.140
of tips for language learning.

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:25.169
So I thought it would be interesting
to go through these 25 tips.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:30.010
And give you my take on these tips,
how relevant, how useful they are.

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:32.890
Bear in mind that language
learning is very personal.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:37.860
So what I like or dislike may not be
the same as what you like or dislike

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:40.279
or what Mark Manson likes or dislike.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:44.210
His first tip is conversation,
conversation, conversation.

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:47.850
Now he was, I think, living in Buenos
Aires and faced with the difficulty

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:49.440
of communicating with locals.

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:53.650
So in that situation, if you're in a
country where the language is spoken

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:57.170
and you need to learn that language,
of course, you take advantage of

00:00:57.170 --> 00:00:59.500
every opportunity to use the language.

00:00:59.519 --> 00:01:03.239
However, as a strategy for
learning the language, I have

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:06.599
never considered conversation,
at least in the initial stages.

00:01:06.855 --> 00:01:11.215
to be the key because you can't have
a conversation if you don't understand

00:01:11.385 --> 00:01:13.095
what the other person is saying.

00:01:13.165 --> 00:01:19.375
So I am much more inclined to prefer heavy
input based approach to language learning

00:01:19.625 --> 00:01:24.034
and wait until you have a high enough
level of comprehension to where you can

00:01:24.034 --> 00:01:26.075
have adult conversations with people.

00:01:26.145 --> 00:01:29.075
And at that point, yes,
you do need to speak a lot.

00:01:29.125 --> 00:01:33.144
The second thing he says is intensity
of study trumps length of study.

00:01:33.385 --> 00:01:34.965
Well, intensity, yes.

00:01:35.355 --> 00:01:38.905
And I do remember when I was learning
Mandarin Chinese, I had three hours a

00:01:38.905 --> 00:01:43.054
day, one on one with the teacher, and
then I would spend five or six or seven

00:01:43.054 --> 00:01:47.205
hours on my own listening and reading
and writing and learning characters.

00:01:47.585 --> 00:01:53.704
And I learned in one year faster than
other diplomatic students, as with

00:01:53.704 --> 00:01:55.174
the Canadian government in Hong Kong.

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:57.890
Uh, who took two years
and learned less well.

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:02.500
So I, I remember I came to the conclusion
at that time that creating that white heat

00:02:02.559 --> 00:02:07.850
in your brain that comes from intensity
is very helpful, but we continue to learn.

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:10.199
So even after a period
of very intense learning.

00:02:10.524 --> 00:02:12.695
It continues to gestate in your brain.

00:02:12.695 --> 00:02:17.045
And there are other opportunities to use
the language and you are always improving

00:02:17.065 --> 00:02:18.584
over a long, long period of time.

00:02:18.694 --> 00:02:23.165
Number three, he says classes suck and
are an inefficient use of time and money.

00:02:23.274 --> 00:02:27.194
One on one as the way I had it
in Hong Kong with a teacher,

00:02:27.194 --> 00:02:28.684
a new teacher every hour.

00:02:28.925 --> 00:02:30.915
Forcing me to say
something in the language.

00:02:30.935 --> 00:02:32.955
I think that was very good, very intense.

00:02:33.125 --> 00:02:36.385
However, sitting in a classroom
with 10 people, with a teacher,

00:02:36.395 --> 00:02:39.674
especially if you have to travel a
half hour or 45 minutes to get to

00:02:39.725 --> 00:02:41.855
the class, the classes are expensive.

00:02:42.065 --> 00:02:43.955
In my case, the government was paying.

00:02:43.994 --> 00:02:46.665
If I had to pay for that myself,
I wouldn't do it because we

00:02:46.665 --> 00:02:48.629
can achieve so much on our own.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:51.180
Listening and reading, and
then eventually finding an

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:53.000
opportunity to use the language.

00:02:53.079 --> 00:02:55.940
Number four, know your motivation
for learning a new language.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:56.790
That's important.

00:02:56.950 --> 00:03:01.100
I was listening to a podcast, How
Stuff Works or something, to Americans.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:02.780
And they were saying, why
would you learn a language?

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:04.370
Is it just for showing off?

00:03:04.799 --> 00:03:07.799
No, that's, I don't think why
most people learn languages,

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:09.760
but it may be the case for some.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:11.500
So you have to look at yourself.

00:03:11.549 --> 00:03:14.010
Am I learning this language to show off?

00:03:14.089 --> 00:03:16.579
Am I learning to communicate
with my grandmother?

00:03:16.779 --> 00:03:18.839
Am I learning it because
I need it for work?

00:03:18.869 --> 00:03:21.189
Am I learning it because I'm
interested in the culture?

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:25.790
It's important to understand why you're
doing it, because there are a lot of

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:29.450
moments during the process where you are
discouraged because you don't think you're

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:33.480
getting anywhere and you have to go back
to like, why am I actually doing it?

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:34.350
So that's a good point.

00:03:34.429 --> 00:03:37.440
Number five, he says set learning goals.

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:42.650
Well, I've said that in a general sense,
my goal always is B2 on the European

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:47.280
Common Framework of Reference, which
is kind of fluent with mistakes and

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:48.489
places where you don't understand.

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:49.480
That to me is the goal.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:50.990
Once you're there, you can continue.

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:54.440
To improve, but I very
often don't achieve that.

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:58.180
I move off a language where I'm
still at A2 and I go to another

00:03:58.180 --> 00:03:59.770
language because I'm interested in it.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:03.860
So to me, at my stage, the
goals aren't that important.

00:04:03.939 --> 00:04:07.870
When I was a Canadian government language
student in Hong Kong, learning Mandarin

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:09.680
Chinese, I had a very specific goal.

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:12.830
I had to write the British
foreign service exam.

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:14.670
And I said, I want to
do that within a year.

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:17.400
So that was a very specific
goal for a specific purpose.

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:19.280
That's not the situation now.

00:04:19.474 --> 00:04:21.604
And the goal will dictate what you do.

00:04:21.644 --> 00:04:23.315
Are you going to spend
a lot of time writing?

00:04:23.325 --> 00:04:24.835
Are you going to maintain a diary?

00:04:24.984 --> 00:04:28.265
All the different things that you
can do, many of which are hard work.

00:04:28.514 --> 00:04:32.775
If your goal is high enough, you will
have the discipline to do these things.

00:04:32.825 --> 00:04:35.405
If you're more of a dilettante
like me, you'll be more of a

00:04:35.414 --> 00:04:37.644
lazy language learner like I am.

00:04:37.794 --> 00:04:42.145
Next thing he says, start with the most
common words, the 100 most common words.

00:04:42.215 --> 00:04:43.375
I see that all the time.

00:04:43.455 --> 00:04:45.615
I have never found that
to be useful advice.

00:04:46.094 --> 00:04:50.335
If you are doing a lot of listening
and reading or even speaking, you

00:04:50.335 --> 00:04:56.794
will encounter the most common 100
words, 500 words very, very often.

00:04:57.145 --> 00:05:01.565
However, the frequency declines very
quickly and the major challenge...

00:05:01.925 --> 00:05:06.215
Thereafter is going to be acquiring
the less frequent words, but the

00:05:06.215 --> 00:05:10.115
most common words they look after
themselves, you needn't worry about it.

00:05:10.205 --> 00:05:13.205
And if your goal is to learn the most
common hundred words so that you can

00:05:13.205 --> 00:05:16.645
go and start talking to someone, you
will find, I think, in many, most

00:05:16.645 --> 00:05:20.044
cases that you won't be able to follow
the conversation anyway, because

00:05:20.044 --> 00:05:23.685
the person you're speaking to is not
going to limit what they have to say

00:05:24.075 --> 00:05:26.025
to the hundred most common words.

00:05:26.115 --> 00:05:28.414
Number seven, he says,
carry a pocket dictionary.

00:05:28.414 --> 00:05:30.164
I never carry a pocket dictionary.

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:32.220
But dictionaries are very inefficient.

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:34.090
It takes time to find a word.

00:05:34.550 --> 00:05:37.790
You forget what the meaning was as
soon as you close the dictionary,

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:40.450
I just find, you know, you end
up saying, how do you say this?

00:05:40.450 --> 00:05:41.210
How do you say that?

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:42.450
Could you please repeat?

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:44.990
And you kind of struggle
through if you have that kind

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:47.069
of a rudimentary conversation.

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:50.349
I prefer to prepare myself through
lots of listening and reading

00:05:50.419 --> 00:05:51.649
and then hope for the best.

00:05:51.659 --> 00:05:53.140
So I don't carry a pocket dictionary.

00:05:53.149 --> 00:05:55.400
Number eight, keep practicing
the new language in your head.

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:55.920
Again, I never...

00:05:56.055 --> 00:05:58.035
Do that, not deliberately.

00:05:58.065 --> 00:06:03.055
Now it may be that subconsciously I
have these phrases bouncing around in

00:06:03.055 --> 00:06:08.215
my head, but I don't deliberately try
to practice the new language in my head.

00:06:08.244 --> 00:06:08.895
I just don't do that.

00:06:09.055 --> 00:06:10.565
That's not to say it's
not a good thing to do.

00:06:10.715 --> 00:06:13.215
It's just not something
that I can, you know, do.

00:06:13.325 --> 00:06:14.875
You're going to say a
lot of stupid things.

00:06:14.875 --> 00:06:15.595
I accept it.

00:06:15.685 --> 00:06:17.435
Yeah, I'm not sure it's stupid, but.

00:06:17.570 --> 00:06:19.790
Uh, we are comfortable
in our own language.

00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:22.810
Typically we feel we are, if we're
adults, we think we're adult.

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:23.840
We think we're intelligent.

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:25.409
We think we can express ourselves.

00:06:25.409 --> 00:06:28.580
And all of a sudden in a new
language, we can't, we make mistakes.

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:31.150
We can't find the words
in a way it's unpleasant.

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:34.650
Although there is this sort of
compensating sense of achievement

00:06:34.689 --> 00:06:38.870
when in fact, you are able to say
something, you are able to understand,

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:40.290
you get this tremendous power.

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:43.849
But yeah, there are certainly periods of
uncertainty and you have to accept that.

00:06:44.275 --> 00:06:47.355
Number 10, he says, figure
out pronunciation patterns.

00:06:47.355 --> 00:06:51.695
And he makes reference to Slavic
languages and Romance languages,

00:06:51.695 --> 00:06:53.225
which have certain patterns.

00:06:53.295 --> 00:06:58.275
I would rather hear say, when it comes
to pronunciation, focus on intonation.

00:06:58.515 --> 00:07:01.664
If you can get the intonation of a
language, the rhythm of the language.

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:04.630
Then the pronunciation is
going to improve, your correct

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:05.990
usage is going to improve.

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:09.850
You should, you know, listen to that
intonation and try to imitate it.

00:07:09.950 --> 00:07:13.130
Number 11, he says, use audio
and online courses for the first

00:07:13.130 --> 00:07:14.700
hundred words and basic grammar.

00:07:14.909 --> 00:07:16.560
Well, I've covered the
first hundred words.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:17.740
I don't think that's important.

00:07:17.750 --> 00:07:19.340
They'll come at you anyway.

00:07:19.449 --> 00:07:22.710
Basic grammar, again, it's hard
to nail down the basic grammar.

00:07:22.710 --> 00:07:25.680
You'll be struggling with basic
grammar for a long, long time.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:27.740
He mentions various apps.

00:07:27.740 --> 00:07:29.630
He doesn't mention LingQ, unfortunately.

00:07:30.034 --> 00:07:34.174
Fine, yeah, there's all kinds of apps
and tools and resources online that

00:07:34.174 --> 00:07:37.715
are going to help you, but the focus
should be on acquiring the language,

00:07:37.775 --> 00:07:42.135
not necessarily just the first hundred
words, not necessarily the basic grammar.

00:07:42.315 --> 00:07:46.164
Both of those things are going to
continue to elude you for quite a while.

00:07:46.325 --> 00:07:49.075
Number twelve, he says, after
the first hundred words, focus

00:07:49.075 --> 00:07:50.524
on becoming conversational.

00:07:50.810 --> 00:07:52.500
Again, I don't know what that means.

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:55.390
You are not going to be
conversational with a hundred words.

00:07:55.429 --> 00:07:58.429
Forget the first hundred
words, even forget trying to be

00:07:58.429 --> 00:08:00.289
conversational at an early stage.

00:08:00.299 --> 00:08:04.489
Focus on improving your comprehension,
acquiring vocabulary, speak

00:08:04.489 --> 00:08:06.099
when you have the opportunity.

00:08:06.189 --> 00:08:09.989
You're not going to be comfortably
conversational until you have a sufficient

00:08:10.039 --> 00:08:12.030
vocabulary and a sufficient level.

00:08:12.385 --> 00:08:15.705
Of comprehension, he's just
aiming for the brain melt.

00:08:15.935 --> 00:08:16.275
Sure.

00:08:16.275 --> 00:08:18.065
I think I understand what he's saying.

00:08:18.075 --> 00:08:23.965
Go at it as intensively as you can, and
you will be engaging with the language.

00:08:23.965 --> 00:08:26.675
It's kind of occupying
your every waking moment.

00:08:26.744 --> 00:08:29.955
And interestingly, whatever you've
done before going to bed or during the

00:08:29.964 --> 00:08:34.094
day, during the sleeping hours, that
is being reinforced and put into your.

00:08:34.539 --> 00:08:35.450
Long term memory.

00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:40.120
So the more intense that experience,
the bigger the brain melt, I guess, the

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:42.000
faster you're socking it into your brain.

00:08:42.230 --> 00:08:45.620
Use a new language daily only
in the sense that listening and

00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:47.550
reading are also using the language.

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:50.899
It's simply not practical
to be speaking daily.

00:08:51.079 --> 00:08:55.810
But you should try to maintain some
consistency of engaging with the language.

00:08:55.930 --> 00:08:57.900
And so that's certainly good advice.

00:08:57.979 --> 00:08:58.569
Number 15.

00:08:58.589 --> 00:09:01.589
How do you say X is the most important
sentence you can possibly learn?

00:09:01.639 --> 00:09:02.919
Again, I don't agree with that.

00:09:02.979 --> 00:09:04.780
How do you say X is not difficult to say?

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:07.949
You will learn that early,
but it's not a major help.

00:09:08.175 --> 00:09:10.825
I think you'll find yourself
saying, I beg your pardon, could

00:09:10.825 --> 00:09:12.375
you repeat that more often?

00:09:12.585 --> 00:09:16.765
Because when we speak, we tend
to limit ourselves to what we can

00:09:16.774 --> 00:09:18.834
say and the words that we have.

00:09:18.975 --> 00:09:22.765
Number 16, he says, one on one tutoring is
the best and most efficient use of time.

00:09:23.135 --> 00:09:28.565
It's the best and most efficient type of
tutoring or classroom learning one on one.

00:09:28.575 --> 00:09:34.235
I don't believe in sitting with a group
of five or six or ten and taking turns.

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:39.230
Conversing, some are better than
others, not an efficient use of time,

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:44.240
but I think that listening, reading, or
dare I say using LingQ can be at least

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:48.650
as efficient a use of time as one on
one tutoring, but you need a mixture.

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:49.329
You need them all.

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:52.169
The tutor is stimulating, it's feedback.

00:09:52.169 --> 00:09:53.059
It's, it's all good.

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:56.459
Number 17, date someone who
speaks the target language

00:09:56.780 --> 00:09:58.150
and not your native language.

00:09:58.260 --> 00:10:00.199
Uh, I'm married, so it doesn't apply.

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:02.920
You're selectively, you
know, removing anyone who.

00:10:03.305 --> 00:10:06.685
Speaks English, if your native
language is English, harder to find.

00:10:06.695 --> 00:10:09.765
I'm not sure how relevant
that is as a activity.

00:10:09.905 --> 00:10:10.405
18.

00:10:10.415 --> 00:10:13.005
If you can't find someone
cute who will put up with you,

00:10:13.005 --> 00:10:14.415
find a language buddy online.

00:10:14.605 --> 00:10:19.305
So with a language buddy is again, I
have to spend time teaching that person,

00:10:19.305 --> 00:10:23.684
my language, English, so that's an hour
for every hour I get of speaking to them

00:10:23.704 --> 00:10:27.984
in my target language, I would rather
spend more time with input activities.

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:35.210
And then pay 10, 15, 20 an hour
for an online tutor when I want.

00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:38.080
Facebook chat plus Google
Translate equals winning.

00:10:38.130 --> 00:10:39.559
Again, I'm not on Facebook.

00:10:39.559 --> 00:10:41.510
I don't have any interest in Facebook.

00:10:41.690 --> 00:10:45.540
Uh, Google Translate is useful
for written communication.

00:10:45.580 --> 00:10:46.819
I think it's a little cumbersome.

00:10:49.395 --> 00:10:52.585
Number 20, when you learn a new word,
try to use it a few times right away.

00:10:52.625 --> 00:10:56.515
Again, a big part of language learning
to me is that it'd be natural.

00:10:56.615 --> 00:11:00.095
I acquire so many words
through listening and reading.

00:11:00.134 --> 00:11:02.405
I can't possibly use them all right away.

00:11:02.424 --> 00:11:04.095
There's no opportunity to use them.

00:11:04.305 --> 00:11:08.445
There's a gradual process where words
that we have seen and we're starting

00:11:08.445 --> 00:11:11.445
to become familiar with eventually
become passive vocabulary and

00:11:11.445 --> 00:11:13.125
eventually become active vocabulary.

00:11:13.195 --> 00:11:16.510
I don't think you can short
circuit the The process by

00:11:16.510 --> 00:11:17.840
trying to use them right away.

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:22.370
And if you do, you'll only slow down
your acquisition of a richer and

00:11:22.370 --> 00:11:26.980
richer passive vocabulary, which is a
base that you need for comprehension.

00:11:27.230 --> 00:11:31.139
And it's the base upon which all
other language skills are built.

00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:33.200
So no, don't try to use it right away.

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:36.350
21, he says, TV shows, movies,
newspapers, magazines are good

00:11:36.350 --> 00:11:41.020
supplements, but he says ultimately
nothing compares to the benefits of.

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:45.430
Speaking like conversations again, I
don't agree with that because if you're

00:11:45.430 --> 00:11:49.890
into TV shows, movies, newspapers, and
magazines, you're expanding your horizons,

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:53.460
getting used to that new language
and everything associated with it.

00:11:53.740 --> 00:11:59.140
If you limit yourself to what you can say,
it becomes a much more limited experience.

00:11:59.170 --> 00:12:01.820
And I don't think that's
a good longterm strategy.

00:12:01.910 --> 00:12:03.870
Number 22, most people are helpful.

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:04.450
Let them help.

00:12:04.790 --> 00:12:08.729
Yeah, most people are helpful, but that
French girl who went to Norway, she

00:12:08.729 --> 00:12:10.020
makes the point and it's very important.

00:12:10.435 --> 00:12:13.035
You know, make sure you're
good before you get there.

00:12:13.334 --> 00:12:16.095
So if you go to Norway and you
aren't very good in Norwegian.

00:12:16.475 --> 00:12:20.944
As helpful as I am sure the Norwegians
are, they're going to speak to you in

00:12:20.954 --> 00:12:24.655
English because they think they're being
helpful by speaking to you in English.

00:12:24.865 --> 00:12:27.425
So you can't treat
random people as tutors.

00:12:27.995 --> 00:12:32.475
You have to get yourself to a level where
people are comfortable speaking to you in

00:12:32.475 --> 00:12:34.154
the language that you're trying to learn.

00:12:34.314 --> 00:12:38.464
Number 23, there will be a lot of
ambiguity and miscommunication.

00:12:38.464 --> 00:12:39.645
Yes, of course there will.

00:12:40.485 --> 00:12:44.525
It comes with the territory, as I
mentioned earlier, that, uh, uncertainty

00:12:44.575 --> 00:12:47.635
that's part of learning a language
and we shouldn't let it bother us.

00:12:47.765 --> 00:12:50.965
24, these are the phases you go
through to learn a foreign language.

00:12:50.965 --> 00:12:53.125
I mean, you can describe
the phases any way you want.

00:12:53.295 --> 00:12:57.315
In my view, we have initial
success because from zero, suddenly

00:12:57.315 --> 00:12:58.464
we understand a few things.

00:12:58.684 --> 00:12:59.929
We're able to say a few things.

00:13:00.109 --> 00:13:02.969
Then we go through a phase where we
feel we're not making any progress

00:13:02.969 --> 00:13:07.229
whatsoever because there's so many
low frequency words that we need in

00:13:07.229 --> 00:13:10.439
order to understand a movie, in order
to have meaningful conversations.

00:13:10.729 --> 00:13:13.629
And then all of a sudden we turn
a corner and we have this feeling

00:13:13.630 --> 00:13:15.689
of, wow, I'm somewhere, I'm B2.

00:13:15.939 --> 00:13:19.490
And these phases may be different
for different people, but there is

00:13:19.490 --> 00:13:23.030
a gradual process of working towards
success, however we define it.

00:13:23.315 --> 00:13:26.305
And finally, he says, number
25, find a way to make it fun.

00:13:26.765 --> 00:13:30.815
I would not put that as finally,
I would put that as upfront.

00:13:30.995 --> 00:13:35.125
Figure out a way to enjoy the
process and at every stage.

00:13:35.135 --> 00:13:37.645
And we talked about
stages, enjoy the process.

00:13:37.655 --> 00:13:43.015
So I, for example, at an early stage,
uh, I find it enjoyable to listen to

00:13:43.015 --> 00:13:44.565
our mini stories over and over again.

00:13:44.855 --> 00:13:48.245
Comes a point where I can no
longer do that because we need

00:13:48.245 --> 00:13:50.055
repetition, but we need new things.

00:13:50.370 --> 00:13:54.530
And so I find it enjoyable to move
onto things that are difficult, where

00:13:54.530 --> 00:13:58.290
I'm meeting some of the same words and
phrases, but in many different contexts,

00:13:58.490 --> 00:14:03.050
and I'm learning about many things through
the language, and I find that enjoyable.

00:14:03.169 --> 00:14:06.769
Obviously, when I have an opportunity to
use the language, if I'm in the country,

00:14:06.769 --> 00:14:10.480
or if I hear someone speaking a language
that I've been learning, I'll immediately

00:14:10.490 --> 00:14:12.120
pounce on them and speak to them.

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:13.510
And so that's also enjoyable.

00:14:13.915 --> 00:14:18.735
So it's important at every stage
to find ways to enjoy the process,

00:14:19.064 --> 00:14:23.715
not just finally, but even at the
very beginning, everyone has their

00:14:23.715 --> 00:14:25.474
own approach to language learning.

00:14:25.475 --> 00:14:27.835
If it works for them,
that's all that matters.

00:14:27.944 --> 00:14:32.004
And I'm going to leave with you two
videos that I would suggest you have a

00:14:32.004 --> 00:14:36.115
look at talking about the importance of
input, because I think that's crucial.

00:14:36.205 --> 00:14:39.525
And that kind of underlies my
philosophy of language learning.

00:14:39.585 --> 00:14:40.365
Thanks for listening.

00:14:40.375 --> 00:14:40.904
Bye for now.

